"""
Palacio Real de Aranjuez (Q530129) — the palace with the Parterre and the Jardín de la Isla, built by code.

Author: Álvaro C. Chulvi (with AI assistance). Licence of the resulting model: CC BY 4.0.
The building: Juan Bautista de Toledo (design 1564, died 1567) and Juan de Herrera (died 1597) for Philip II;
finished for Ferdinand VI by Santiago Bonavía (main façade 1748–1752, died 1759); the two wings of the Plaza de
Armas by Francesco Sabatini for Charles III (1771–1777, died 1797). The Parterre was laid out by Étienne Boutelou
for Philip V (1728, redrawn in the 19th century); the Jardín de la Isla is of the 16th–17th centuries; the
fountain of Hercules and Antaeus is by Isidro González Velázquez (1827, died 1840). Public domain.

What is modelled:
- The palace: the west range on the Plaza de Armas with its central body (five-arch portico, the attic with the
  arms of Ferdinand VI, the curved pediment and the statues of Philip II, Philip V and Ferdinand VI), the two
  corner towers with their drums, lead domes, lanterns and crosses, the square block round the courtyard to the
  east, Sabatini's two wings with the arcades on the plaza and the three arches at their ends, the balustrades
  with urns, slate hipped roofs and chimneys, ~1,000 windows and balconies. Red brick panels framed in white
  Colmenar stone.
- The Parterre (east): its beds with their box edging as OpenStreetMap draws them, the gravel walks, the
  fountains of Hercules and Antaeus (round pond with the group on its pedestal), Ceres and the two Nereids, the
  ha-ha moat with its stone walls along the east and south sides, the Jardín del Rey and the Jardín de la Reina.
- The Jardín de la Isla (north, across the Ría): the part within ~330 m of the palace — the hedged blocks
  (bosquets) with their plane trees, the lawns, the gravel walks, the fountains of Hercules and the Hydra (with
  its four ponds), Apollo, the Boticaria, the Clock (Horas), the Niño de la Espina and Venus, the two small
  bridges over the Ría, and the Salón de los Reyes Católicos, the avenue of plane trees along the Tagus.
- Not modelled: the Tagus and the Ría (Offlined draws the water), the rest of the island garden further west,
  the Casa de Oficios and Casa de Caballeros, the interiors.

Courtyards (since 2026-11-28): the open courtyards the building encloses get a floor (tools/ground_fill.py), coloured
from the IGN orthophoto (PNOA, © Instituto Geográfico Nacional de España, CC BY 4.0, scne.es; model_sources/Q530129_pnoa.jpg,
its baked shadows taken out); before, the game saw through them and drew its own ground.
Measurements (see meta.json for the sources):
- Plan: OpenStreetMap relation 2610430 (outline and courtyard), in the axes of heading 273.7° (the main front
  faces west onto the Plaza de Armas). Main block 110.7 m north–south along the plaza front, 85.7 m deep; the
  courtyard 26.4 × 26.4 m; the wings 18.5–19.7 m wide and 77 m long; the plaza between them 82.6 m wide.
- Heights from photographs on Wikimedia Commons, scaled on the plan (±10 %): ranges 17 m to the cornice and
  21.5 m to the ridge; central body 24.5 m to the attic cornice, 27.5 m to the top of the pediment, statues to
  30 m; corner towers 24 m, drum to 29 m, dome to 33.5 m, lantern and cross to 40.5 m; wings 12.8 m to the
  cornice, 13.9 m to the balustrade.
- Gardens, beds, ponds, fountains, moat, bridges, the island's outline: OpenStreetMap ways (ids in meta.json).
Ground: Aranjuez lies flat on the floor of the Tagus valley (≈ 490 m): y = 0 is the Plaza de Armas.

Axes (docs/MODEL_SPEC.md): +Y up, the main (west) front faces +Z (heading 273.7°), +X points to 183.7° (south).
Origin at the centroid of the OSM outline of the palace (in the west range).

    python model_sources/Q530129_palacio_real_de_aranjuez.py   -> models/Q530129/model.glb (+ footprint in meta.json)
"""
